  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "For an array AAA of length NNN, we define f(A)f(A)f(A) to be the sum of GCDs of its adjacent elements, i.e.\n\nf(A)=∑i=1N−1gcd⁡(Ai,Ai+1)f(A) = \\sum_{i=1}^{N-1} \\gcd(A_i, A_{i+1})f(A)=i=1∑N−1​gcd(Ai​,Ai+1​)You are given two integers NNN and KKK.\n\nFind any array AAA of length NNN, consisting of **distinct integers** between 111 and 10910^9109, such that f(A)=Kf(A) = Kf(A)=K.\n\nIf multiple valid arrays exist, you may find any of them.\n\nIf no valid arrays exist, print −1-1−1 instead.\n\n\nInput Format\n\nThe first line of input will contain a single integer TTT, denoting the number of test cases.\nThe first and only line of each test case contains two space-separated integers NNN and KKK – the length of the array and the required value of f(A)f(A)f(A), respectively.\n\n\nOutput Format\nFor each test case, on a new line:\n\n\n\nIf no valid array exists, print the single integer −1-1−1.\nOtherwise, output NNN space-separated integers A1,A2,…,ANA_1, A_2, \\ldots, A_NA1​,A2​,…,AN​, representing the array you found.\n\nEach AiA_iAi​ should be an integer between 111 and 10910^9109, and f(A)=Kf(A) = Kf(A)=K must hold. The elements of AAA must be pairwise distinct, i.e. Ai≠AjA_i \\neq A_jAi​=Aj​ for i≠ji \\neq ji=j.\n\nIf there are multiple valid arrays, you may find any of them.",
    "constraints": [
      "1≤T≤1051 \\leq T \\leq 10^51≤T≤105",
      "2≤N≤2⋅1052 \\leq N \\leq 2\\cdot 10^52≤N≤2⋅105",
      "1≤K≤1061 \\leq K \\leq 10^61≤K≤106",
      "The sum of NNN over all test cases won't exceed 2⋅1052\\cdot 10^52⋅105."
    ],
    "inputFormat": "",
    "outputFormat": "",
    "notes": [],
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "3\n3 5\n5 3\n5 18",
        "output": "15 54 8\n-1\n9 210 72 30 57",
        "explanation": "Explanation:\nTest case 111: We have A=[15,54,8]A = [15, 54, 8]A=[15,54,8], for which f(A)=gcd⁡(15,54)+gcd⁡(54,8)=3+2=5f(A) = \\gcd(15, 54) + \\gcd(54, 8) = 3 + 2 = 5f(A)=gcd(15,54)+gcd(54,8)=3+2=5, as required.\nTest case 222: It can be proved that there does not exist any array of length 555, with distinct integers between 111 and 10910^9109, such that the sum of adjacent GCDs equals 333.\nTest case 333: We have f(A)=gcd⁡(9,210)+gcd⁡(210,72)+gcd⁡(72,30)+gcd⁡(30,57)=3+6+6+3=18f(A) = \\gcd(9, 210) + \\gcd(210, 72) + \\gcd(72, 30) + \\gcd(30, 57) = 3 + 6 + 6 + 3 = 18f(A)=gcd(9,210)+gcd(210,72)+gcd(72,30)+gcd(30,57)=3+6+6+3=18."
      }
    ]
  },
